here in the United States we have what I  would describe as an employer connected  for-profit health insurance system and  there's nothing sort of natural or  obvious about that system we could have  had a different system all along and  actually in his book deadly spin wendell  potter talks about this how I think it  was between World War 1 and World War 2  we were starting to think about maybe  not going in the direction that we went  and maybe going with something more like  a universal or government health care  program like many of our European  counterparts have that was aborted  because it was too similar to what at  the time Germany was doing our enemy at  the time Germany and so we got the  system that we currently have in the  1990s we started seeing these proposals  from right-wing think tanks for what we  ultimately ended up with in Obamacare  which is a mandate that everybody get  insurance but from for-profit private  insurance companies it didn't happen in  the 1990s but eventually it did with  Obamacare so this is really a right-wing  system people like to say Oh Obama's  Obamacare is a government takeover it's  socialism it's communism there's really  nothing communistic or socialistic from  mandating that everybody become  customers of for-profit insurance  companies so to begin with the  Affordable Care Act is pretty damn  right-wing the problem now for Donald  Trump and Republicans is that they  campaigned for over a year on repealing  and replacing Obamacare and made totally  impossible promises about what the end  result would be you saw dozens and  dozens of symbolic votes in the house  under the presidency of Barack Obama to  repeal the Affordable Care Act which  wouldn't have made a difference it was a  waste of time because it wasn't going to  pass the Senate and if it did President  Obama would have vetoed it anyway and  all of a sudden now you've got  Republicans in the White House  controlling the Senate and controlling  the house and they need to make good on  the campaign promise they said they  would repeal the Affordable Care Act  they said everyone will be covered it  will be cheaper for everyone care will  be better and that's proving to be  impossible and it's not been going well  the first  version of the Obamacare replacement the  American Health Care Act according to  the CBO would have seen 24 million  people lose their coverage the next  version from the house would have seen  23 million people lose their health  insurance coverage we now have a scoring  from the Congressional Budget Office of  the Senate version 2 of the American  Health Care Act which would see an  estimated 22 million people lose  coverage some because they would choose  not to have coverage by the removal of  the mandate no doubt but in total we  would see 22 million fewer people  covered so Mitch McConnell said we want  to vote before the fourth of July break  that starts this Friday we now have  learned not going to happen Mitch  McConnell throwing in the towel saying  we've got to go back to the drawing  board we need to make changes we need to  make amendment it's not going to happen  before the fourth of July break and to  be perfectly clear this is not failing  Pat because of Democratic obstruction  this is Republicans who can't get 50  votes together in the Senate and what  does this prove this proves that they  now realize they can't do what was  promised and what it would have real  political implications if they did right  we knew for a long time that it would be  hard for Republicans to get the votes  together because you had people like  Murkowski and Collins opposing it from  the center and then you had people like  Lee and Cruz opposing it from the right  now you have three or four more names  added on to that and they have to get to  50 votes they can only afford to lose  two it's not even close right now and  Donald Trump held a meeting yesterday  with a bunch of senators and that this  was before it was announced there just  isn't going to be a vote it's just not  going to happen this week he also  tweeted about this saying quote some of  the fake news media likes to say that I  am not totally engaged in health care  wrong I know the subject well and want  victory for us for the u.s. it's almost  worse that Trump is admitting that he's  heavily involved because it's been such  a failure I would want to hide the fact  that I've been involved with this if I  were Trump blame Republicans and  Republicans alone or blame Democrats for  obstruction like he's been doing  although it makes no sense  it can only get worse if you move to the  right  you can't possibly start with Obamacare  and move to the right  giving the for-profit companies more  freedom to raise prices etc and do all  the things you promised right think  about it if you let insurance companies  charge more  some people will no longer be able to  afford insurance they're going to get  cut off from health care you have these  Obamacare subsidies if you reduce the  Obamacare subsidies as the new version  of the Senate bill wants to do then some  people aren't going to be able to afford  insurance they are going to lose care  you're breaking the promise that Donald  Trump made you could say well we'll let  companies keep costs the same for most  people by charging people with  pre-existing conditions more well then  those people won't be able to afford  care and some of those people are going  to die statistically speaking you can't  move to the right and keep cost the same  or lower while keeping coverage the same  or higher you can't but here's the thing  I want people to understand that this  isn't over right now Republicans are  going to make some changes they'll  probably get a slightly better scoring  from the CBO and they're going to try  again so now is when you've got to  contact your Senators and your members  of the house right exactly and people  have to understand that there is an  alternative to the Affordable Care Act  but the sensible one is not to move to  the right we have you just bill has a  seventeen percent approval rating how  about we propose something like Medicare  for all single pet single payer well I'm  going to talk a little bit later on in  the show about that in the context of  Bernie Sanders the progressive movement  senator Elizabeth Warren I've got a lot  to say about that and and I think you're  absolutely right Pat that there needs to  be a paradigm shift on the left but  we'll get to that a little bit later
 

